To start the Timewave Zero software one of four batch files should be
run, depending on your system configuration.  Type the name of the
appropriate batch file at the DOS prompt as follows:

If you have no graphics adaptor installed (i.e. if you have a monochrome
monitor with no graphics card) then type TWZMONO.
If you have a CGA, EGA or VGA adaptor installed then:
(i)   If you have an 80x87 math coprocessor installed then type TWZ87.
(ii)  If you have no 80x87 math coprocessor installed then type TWZ.
(iii) If you have a laser printer connected then type TWZLP.

If you are using an IBM PS/2 computer and this does not work then type
"GRAPHICS" at the DOS prompt and then "TWZERO" or (with a math
coprocessor) "TWZERO87".

If your system has a graphics card installed and a math coprocessor
then you can also start the software directly by typing TWZERO87 at
the DOS prompt.  If your system has a graphics card installed and no
math coprocessor then you can also start the software by typing TWZERO.

When the program starts it will display the title screen.  Press a key
and the main screen will be displayed, with "Screen no. 11". This should
display the current date and time, read from the system clock.  Press
"F" to graph the timewave for the 7-day period centered on the current date.

The program provides eleven screens, so that you can have eleven
different graphs available.  You move among the screens using the PgUp
and PgDn screens or the function keys (F1 - F11).  When the screens are
saved or loaded as a set only the first ten screens are saved or loaded.

The manner of dumping the Timewave Zero graph to the printer may be
changed (if necessary) by modifying the GRAPHICS installation command
in TWZ.BAT.  If you are using DOS 5.0 then the graph may be dumped to
a wider variety of printers (including the Hewlett-Packard Laserjet
printers).  Consult your DOS manual concerning the GRAPHICS command.

The value of SEEKFACTOR in the TWZ.CFG file is 3, meaning that in an
automatic seek for a maximum or a minumum the software expands or
contracts the timespan by a factor of 3.  The value of this factor
affects how long it takes to find the maximum or minumum.  A value of
3 is suitable for 80286 and 80386 machines. If you are running the
software on an 8086 machine then you may prefer to set SEEKFACTOR=4 in
the TWZ.CFG file.  On an 80486 machine SEEKFACTOR=2 might be preferable.

Please consult Chapter 2 of the manual for full details of operation.

The DFR.EXE program is a separate utilty that can be used to browse
textfiles, e.g. DFR *.DOC.

